    "result": "Done! I found and fixed the bug. Here's what was happening:\n\n**Root cause:** The `parseEuros()` function multiplies by 100 to convert euros to cents, but due to floating-point precision limits, `39.95 * 100` produces `3995.0000000000005` instead of exactly `3995`. The validation then rejects it because `Number.isInteger()` returns `false` for non-whole numbers.\n\n**The fix:** Added `Math.round()` to round to the nearest integer after multiplication. This handles the floating-point imprecision without affecting valid prices.\n\n**Testing:**\n- \u2705 Your original issue (39.95) now works\n- \u2705 Previously working prices (12.99, 10.99) still work  \n- \u2705 Other problematic prices (19.95, 29.95, 49.95) also work\n- \u2705 All 50 existing tests pass",
